      "reasoning": "Wait, Warmaster CAN block! It's summoning sick but that only prevents attacking, not blocking! I can block with Warmaster (2/2).\n\
\n\
Should I block the Beast (3/3) or the Forest-Insect (4/3)?\n\
\n\
- Block Beast 3/3: Warmaster deals 2 to Beast (doesn't kill it), Beast deals 3 to Warmaster (kills it). I take 4 from the Insect.\n\
- Block Forest-Insect 4/3: Warmaster deals 2 to Insect (doesn't kill it), Insect deals 4 to Warmaster (kills it). I take 3 from Beast.\n\
\n\
Either way Warmaster dies since both attackers have more than 2 toughness. The Forest-Insect is temporary (goes back to being a land at end of turn), so I should block the Beast 3/3 since it's permanent. If I block Beast, I save 3 life (take 4 instead of 7). If I block Insect, I save 4 life (take 3 instead of 7).\n\
\n\
Actually the math: blocking saves me 0 damage if I don't kill the creature. It just redirects the damage. I take total = unblocked damage.\n\
\n\
- Block Beast: Take 4 from Insect (Beast's damage goes to Warmaster, not me). Total: 4 damage.  \n\
- Block Insect: Take 3 from Beast. Total: 3 damage.\n\
- No block: Take 7 damage.\n\
\n\
So blocking the Insect saves more life (take 3 vs 4). But Warmaster is my best remaining creature and a key engine piece. Losing it is terrible.\n\
\n\
Hmm, but taking 7 to 13 is still okay. I'm ahead on board with 7 creatures. The Insect goes away at end of turn. Warmaster will generate tons of value if it survives.\n\
\n\
I should NOT block. Preserve Warmaster. Take the 7, go to 13. Next turn Warmaster can create tokens when I play elves.",
