      "reasoning": "ELVISH ARCHDRUID! This is an INCREDIBLE draw! 2/2 for 1GG — gives all other Elves +1/+1, AND taps for G per Elf you control. With 7 Elves, that's +7 mana!\n\
\n\
Let me think about sequencing:\n\
\n\
**Option A: Band Together first, then Archdruid**\n\
- Band Together (2G, 3 mana): Invoker (4) + Warmaster (2) = 6 damage → kills Rampaging Baloths 6/6\n\
- Then Archdruid (1GG, 3 mana): 2 mana left... wait, I have 5 lands. 3 for Band Together, leaves 2. Archdruid costs 3 (1GG). Can't cast both!\n\
\n\
Unless... Actually I have 5 untapped lands. Band Together = 3 mana. Archdruid = 3 mana. Total = 6. I only have 5 lands. Can't do both.\n\
\n\
**Option B: Archdruid first**\n\
- Cast Archdruid (1GG, 3 mana). All other Elves get +1/+1. Warmaster → 3/3, tokens → 2/2, Rejuvenator → 2/2, Invoker → 5/4 (wait, is Invoker an Elf? Yes! Elf Shaman!). \n\
- Warmaster triggers — creates another Elf token!\n\
- Then Archdruid taps for G per Elf. I'll have tons of mana.\n\
- Then Band Together: Invoker (5) + Warmaster (3) = 8 damage → kills Baloths easily!\n\
- Or even just attack for massive damage!\n\
\n\
Wait, Archdruid has summoning sickness so it can't tap for mana this turn. But the +1/+1 buff is static and immediate.\n\
\n\
After Archdruid resolves + Warmaster token:\n\
- Archdruid 2/2 (sick, doesn't buff itself)\n\
- Warmaster 3/3 (+1/+1 from Archdruid)\n\
- Invoker 5/4 (+1/+1)  \n\
- Rejuvenator 2/2 (+1/+1)\n\
- 5 Elf tokens at 2/2 (+1/+1)\n\
- Total creatures: 9, attackable (non-sick): 7 (Warmaster 3, Invoker 5, Rejuvenator 2, 4 tokens at 2 each = 18 power)\n\
\n\
Opponent has 8 life and 3 blockers (Baloths 6/6, Baloth 4/3, Cultivator 0/3). If they block 3 of my attackers, I still push through massive damage.\n\
\n\
With 18 power attacking and them at 8 life... Even if they block optimally (Baloths blocks Invoker 5/4, Canopy Baloth blocks Warmaster 3/3, Cultivator blocks a 2/2 token), that's 3 creatures blocked. Remaining: Rejuvenator (2) + 3 tokens (2+2+2) = 8 damage. EXACTLY lethal!\n\
\n\
But wait, Baloths has trample — it doesn't matter since it's blocking. And Warmaster (3/3) vs Canopy Baloth (4/3) — Warmaster deals 3 (kills Baloth), Baloth deals 4 (kills Warmaster). Invoker (5/4) vs Baloths (6/6) — Invoker deals 5 (not enough to kill 6/6), Baloths deals 6 (kills Invoker).\n\
\n\
So unblocked: 4 creatures = 8 damage = LETHAL! If they block 3 and let 4 through, each at 2/2, that's 8 damage exactly lethal on 8 life!\n\
\n\
But they could block differently — block smaller creatures to prevent some damage and take less. Let me think:\n\
- They block Invoker (saves 5 damage), a token (saves 2), and another token (saves 2) = saves 9 damage. I deal 18-9 = 9. That's more than 8, still lethal!\n\
\n\
Actually any 3 blocks from them saves at most... Let me think about it from the other side. 18 power attacking. They have 3 blockers. Maximum damage prevented = Invoker (5) + Warmaster (3) + token (2) = 10. I deal 8. Exactly lethal!\n\
\n\
Or if they block the three biggest: Invoker (5) + Warmaster (3) + Rejuvenator (2) = 10 prevented. 18-10 = 8. STILL LETHAL!\n\
\n\
Actually, they could block differently. If they block Invoker (5), one token (2), one token (2) = prevent 9. I deal 9. Overkill.\n\
\n\
Best blocking for them: Block 3 creatures to maximize damage prevented. Top 3 attackers by power: Invoker (5), Warmaster (3), Rejuvenator/token (2). Block those = prevent 10. Remaining: 4 tokens × 2 = 8. EXACTLY 8 = LETHAL!\n\
\n\
Wait, do they actually die? 8 damage against 8 life = exactly dead. YES!\n\
\n\
So no matter how they block (with 3 blockers), I deal at least 8 damage which is exactly lethal! This is a guaranteed win!\n\
\n\
Let me play Archdruid first.",
